6.2 Clinic entity
Each HCP may have one or multiple clinic locations.
For the current workbook, support at least Clinic 1 and Clinic 2.
Each clinic should have:
- Internal clinic ID.
- Linked HCP ID.
- Clinic label.
- Zone or area.
- Original location text.
- Original Google Maps URL, when provided.
- Verified latitude and longitude.
- Geocoding status.
- Weekly availability rules.
- Optional clinic-specific visit duration.
- Active or inactive status.
- Location verification date.
Do not assume that a clinic's zone name is an exact geographic address.
Do not infer precise coordinates from a vague zone label. Use an approved geocoding provider or ask the user to confirm the location.
When a Google Maps URL is present, extract coordinates only when the URL format or an approved service provides them reliably. Otherwise, open the location for confirmation.
6.3 One HCP, multiple clinics
A single HCP with two clinics is still one HCP for quarterly coverage purposes.
The optimizer must select a feasible clinic for each planned visit based on:
- Clinic availability.
- Travel distance and traffic.
- Visit duration.
- Geographic grouping.
- Existing visit history.
- Remaining quarterly requirement.
- User preferences and fixed appointments.
Visiting Clinic 1 and Clinic 2 must not automatically count as two separate required HCP visits on the same day.
Prevent accidental double-counting of one HCP in a daily plan unless the user explicitly permits multiple qualifying visits and the business rules support them.

7. AVAILABILITY SCHEDULE PARSER
This module is essential because visiting schedules in my Excel file are written in free text.
Examples of source schedules include:
- Saturday and Monday 2-6 PM
- Evening from 8-9 PM EVERY DAY
- Daily 8:30-10 PM except Monday and Friday
- Sat, Mon, Wed, Thu 4-10 PM
- Sunday and Tuesday 7 PM-10 PM
Build a robust schedule parser that converts these descriptions into structured recurring weekly availability windows.
7.1 Parsing requirements
Recognize:
- Full weekday names.
- Common weekday abbreviations.
- Multiple weekdays in one sentence.
- AM and PM.
- Common time formats, including 24-hour time.
- “Every day,” “daily,” and equivalent recurring expressions.
- “Except” and excluded weekdays.
- Multiple time windows per day.
- Common punctuation and spacing variations.
- Commonly encountered spelling errors when the intended meaning is sufficiently clear.
For each parsed schedule, retain:
- Original schedule text.
- Parsed weekdays.
- Start and end times.
- Parsing status.
- Confidence or ambiguity status.
- User confirmation status.
- Date of last verification.
7.2 Safety against incorrect interpretation
Do not treat an uncertain interpretation as confirmed availability.
If the parser cannot confidently determine a weekday, time, AM/PM, or exception, flag the schedule and ask the user to correct it.
For example, a schedule containing 8-10 without AM or PM should be marked ambiguous unless surrounding context provides a reliable interpretation.
Handle time ranges crossing noon or midnight explicitly. Do not assume that every end time earlier than a start time represents an overnight clinic session.
Store recurring availability as local-time rules using the configured territory time zone.
Support temporary exceptions, holidays, and one-off appointments separately from recurring weekly schedules.
7.3 Availability editor
Build a weekly calendar editor where I can:
- Select a clinic.
- View its availability by weekday.
- Add or remove time windows.
- Change start and end times.
- Mark a day as unavailable.
- Confirm or reject imported interpretations.
- Correct the schedule without editing the Excel file.
- Review which schedules remain unverified.
Preserve verified schedules when importing future versions of the workbook unless the source data has materially changed.
If a source schedule changes, display the difference and ask whether to accept the new schedule.
The route optimizer must use structured, confirmed availability rules, not raw free text.

8. VISIT FREQUENCY AND COVERAGE MANAGEMENT
The workbook includes Frequency per Quarter.
Interpret a value of 8 as a target of eight qualifying visits per HCP per calendar quarter by default.
Do not interpret it as eight visits per week or eight visits per day.
Make the coverage period and frequency rules configurable.
8.1 Visit ledger
Maintain a visit ledger with:
- Visit ID.
- HCP ID.
- Clinic ID.
- Planned date and time.
- Actual start and end times, when available.
- Visit status.
- Whether the visit qualifies toward the coverage target.
- Optional appropriate professional notes.
- Record source.
- Synchronization status.
- Creation and update timestamps.
Supported statuses should include:
- Planned.
- Completed.
- Cancelled.
- Not completed.
Only qualifying completed visits count toward coverage.
A planned appointment does not count as a completed visit.
A cancelled visit does not count as completed.
Do not automatically count an unsuccessful visit as completed.
8.2 Coverage calculations
For each HCP, calculate:
Remaining visits = max(0, quarterly target - qualifying completed visits in the current quarter)
Also calculate and display:
- Completed visits this quarter.
- Remaining visits.
- Required visit frequency.
- Last completed visit.
- Days since the last visit.
- Overdue status according to configurable coverage rules.
- Coverage risk.
- Projected quarter-end coverage.
Define coverage risk using the remaining target, remaining workdays, available clinic windows, and feasible travel capacity.
Explain how each risk label is calculated.
Do not use an arbitrary risk score without documenting the formula.
8.3 Initial history
The Excel file does not provide a complete visit history.
Therefore, do not silently assume that every HCP has zero completed visits.
Provide an initial setup workflow that allows me to:
- Import historical visits from another file.
- Enter an opening completed-visit count when appropriate.
- Record the applicable quarter and baseline date.
- Mark history as incomplete where necessary.
Clearly distinguish imported history, manually entered baselines, and visits recorded by the app.
