9. DAILY PLANNING ENGINE
The daily planner is the core of the application.
Generate a feasible sequence of HCP visits, including estimated departure times, travel times, arrivals, visit durations, waiting periods, and end-of-day timing.
9.1 Planning inputs
Use:
- Active HCPs.
- Clinic locations and verified coordinates.
- Confirmed clinic availability.
- Quarterly targets and remaining visits.
- Completed visit history.
- HCP and clinic priorities.
- Visit duration.
- Daily target, normally eight visits.
- Maximum duty span of 480 minutes.
- Home location.
- Traffic-aware travel estimates.
- User-defined appointments and locked visits.
- Working days and territory time zone.
- Break settings.
- Excluded HCPs or clinics.
- Optional preferences for starting and finishing times.
Allow the user to choose a planning date.
Allow the user to specify HCPs that must be visited, HCPs that should be prioritized, and HCPs that must be excluded.
9.2 Home location
Allow me to set and verify my home starting point using a map pin, address, or supported location search.
Store the location securely and make it editable.
The app must not require precise location permission simply to use a previously saved home address.
Default to starting from home.
If return-home routing is enabled, calculate a route back home and include the return journey in the duty-span calculation.
9.3 Flexible working hours
Do not assume a fixed 9 AM to 5 PM schedule.
Choose a start time that permits the best feasible plan within HCP availability, configurable working-day rules, and the maximum duty span.
If an HCP is only available in the evening, the planner may start later when this results in a better feasible plan.
Do not split the workday into disconnected shifts.
Do not force visits outside their confirmed availability windows.
9.4 Visit feasibility
For each proposed stop, calculate:
- Departure time from the previous stop.
- Estimated travel time.
- Arrival time.
- Waiting time until the clinic becomes available.
- Planned visit start time.
- Visit duration.
- Visit end time.
- Travel to the next stop.
- Remaining duty time.
A visit is feasible only when all relevant constraints are respected.
For a clinic with availability from 14:00 to 18:00 and a 30-minute visit duration, the visit must start early enough to finish within the permitted window unless a verified business rule explicitly allows otherwise.
Avoid scheduling a visit that technically starts during a clinic window but finishes after the window closes.
Waiting time is permitted when useful but must be calculated and displayed.
9.5 Daily visit target
Try to schedule eight distinct qualifying HCP visits per day by default.
If eight visits are infeasible, return the best feasible plan and explain why the target could not be reached.
For example:
- Only six eligible HCPs have feasible availability.
- Travel time is too high.
- The eight-hour duty span is exceeded.
- Important locations or schedules remain unverified.
- Fixed appointments prevent a feasible sequence.
Never fabricate visits, hide infeasibility, or violate hard constraints to reach the target.

10. WEEKLY PLANNING ENGINE
Generate a weekly plan across configured working days.
The weekly planner must optimize not only each day's route but also the distribution of visits across the week.
Consider:
- Quarterly coverage gaps.
- Remaining visit requirements.
- Clinic availability on each day.
- Geographic clustering.
- Daily visit capacity.
- Fixed appointments.
- Previously completed visits.
- Fairness and coverage risk.
- Avoiding unnecessary repeat visits.
- Avoiding excessive travel between distant areas.
Do not create a weekly plan simply by repeating the same daily plan.
10.1 Weekly objectives
Prioritize:
1. Satisfying hard availability and duty constraints.
2. Meeting mandatory appointments.
3. Improving HCP coverage and reducing overdue visits.
4. Minimizing travel time and distance.
5. Reducing unnecessary waiting.
6. Maintaining a sustainable distribution of work across the week.
Allow the user to lock certain appointments or days and re-optimize the remaining schedule without changing the locked commitments.
Provide a weekly summary of:
- Planned HCP visits.
- Unique HCPs covered.
- Estimated travel time and distance.
- Expected waiting time.
- Duty span per day.
- HCPs not scheduled.
- Quarterly coverage risks.
- Conflicts and unresolved data issues.

11. REAL ROUTE OPTIMIZATION
Implement a real optimization engine rather than relying on a language model to guess the best sequence.
Use Google Maps Platform Routes API or another approved routing service for road-based travel estimates.
Use an appropriate optimization library such as Google OR-Tools, where compatible with the Android architecture, or implement a well-tested constraint solver behind a clean interface.
Do not couple the business logic to a specific provider.
11.1 Optimization objectives
Optimize a configurable weighted objective that considers:
- Total travel time.
- Total travel distance.
- Waiting time.
- HCP priority.
- Remaining quarterly coverage.
- Overdue HCPs.
- Geographic clustering.
- Workload balance across the week.
- Robustness against traffic variability.
- User-locked appointments and preferences.
Use normalized or otherwise carefully scaled objective terms so that one variable does not dominate simply because it uses larger numerical units.
Keep hard constraints separate from soft preferences.
11.2 Hard constraints
The solver must respect:
- Clinic availability.
- Visit duration.
- Maximum 480-minute duty span.
- No split shifts.
- Home starting point.
- Return-home requirement when enabled.
- Locked appointments.
- Excluded clinics and HCPs.
- Valid location and travel data.
- No accidental duplicate qualifying visits.
- Configured rules for required breaks.
A soft preference must never override a hard constraint.
11.3 Traffic-aware routing
Use live traffic-aware travel estimates when the provider supports them.
If live traffic is unavailable, use the following fallback hierarchy:
1. Live traffic-aware route estimate.
2. Fresh cached road travel estimate.
3. Older cached road travel estimate with a visible age indicator.
4. Historical or typical road travel estimate.
5. Clearly labeled approximate estimate, if no road-based estimate is available.
Use straight-line distance only as a last-resort approximation for rough ranking, not as if it were actual driving time.
Do not claim that an offline plan is live-traffic optimized.
Display when traffic data was last updated and identify plans based on stale or approximate estimates.
Use a configurable safety buffer where appropriate to reduce the risk of late arrivals.
11.4 Route matrix efficiency
Avoid requesting a separate route for every possible pair if a route matrix can calculate many travel times more efficiently.
Cache route estimates using appropriate origin, destination, travel mode, departure-time context, and provider settings.
Use suitable cache expiration policies. Never reuse an old traffic estimate as current live traffic.
Handle API errors, quotas, network loss, and rate limits gracefully.

12. ALTERNATIVE PLANS AND VARIATION
The application must not generate the same plan every time merely because it starts from the same initial HCP list.
Generate up to three distinct feasible plans when meaningful alternatives exist.
Examples:
- Plan A: Minimize total travel time.
- Plan B: Improve coverage of overdue or high-priority HCPs.
- Plan C: Improve route robustness and reduce waiting or traffic risk.
These are examples, not mandatory labels. The alternatives must reflect actual differences in the input data and objective functions.
12.1 How to generate alternatives
Use controlled diversification techniques such as:
- Alternative initial solutions.
- Different geographically clustered starting groups.
- Different soft-objective weight combinations.
- Local search and neighborhood variations.
- Different insertion orders for non-locked stops.
- Alternative feasible appointment choices.
Do not use random shuffling as a substitute for optimization.
Do not deliberately make a plan worse just to make it look different.
12.2 Quality and diversity
Evaluate each candidate using:
- Constraint validity.
- Total travel time.
- Total distance.
- Waiting time.
- Priority coverage.
- Quarterly coverage impact.
- Robustness.
- Similarity to other candidates.
Remove duplicate or nearly identical plans.
Prefer high-quality plans that provide a meaningful trade-off.
A configurable initial exploration range, such as plans within approximately 10% of the best feasible objective, may be used to search for alternatives. This is a tuning parameter, not a guarantee and not permission to violate constraints.
If only one good feasible plan exists, explain that rather than manufacturing alternatives.
12.3 Alternative plan comparison
Display a comparison table with:
- Number of planned HCP visits.
- Unique HCPs covered.
- Total driving time.
- Total distance.
- Waiting time.
- Duty span.
- Quarterly coverage benefit.
- Number of high-priority HCPs covered.
- Data confidence and traffic freshness.
- Main advantages and disadvantages.
Allow me to select a plan and continue editing it.

